response:
 If I know the sale price is $60\%$ off the original price and I know the sale price, I can find the original price. 
The sale price is $60\%$ off the original price, so the sale price is $40\%$ of the original price. This is the same as $\dfrac{4}{10}$ of the original price, so if I let $x$ be the original price, then the sale price equals $\dfrac{4x}{10}$. 
I know the sale price is $\$14.40$, so I have the equation
\begin{gather*}
\dfrac{4x}{10} = 14.40,
\end{gather*}
which I can solve for $x$ by multiplying both sides of the equation by $10$ and dividing both sides of the equation by $4$.
\begin{gather*}
\dfrac{4x}{10} \times 10 = 14.40\times 10,
\end{gather*}
which simplifies to
\begin{gather*}
&\dfrac{4x}{10} \times 10 = 14.40\times 10,\\[1ex]
\implies & 4x = 144,
\end{gather*}
which can be solved for $x$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $4$.
\begin{gather*}
\dfrac{4x}{4} = \dfrac{144}{4},
\end{gather*}
which simplifies to
\begin{gather*}
x = 36.
\end{gather*}
That quotient is the original price, which the Question only asked, so the Answer is: $36$

