\begin{align*}
&\cos(\alpha)\cos(\beta) - \sin(\alpha)\sin(\beta) + \sin(\alpha)\cos(\beta) + \cos(\alpha)\sin(\beta) = 0 
\end{align*}
which simplifies to
\begin{align*}
&\cos(\alpha)\cos(\beta) + \cos(\alpha)\sin(\beta) = 0
\end{align*}
Using the given $\tan \beta = \frac{1}{2000}$ and recalling the Pythagorean identity $\tan^2 \beta + 1 = \sec^2 \beta$, we find
\begin{align*}
\cos^2(\beta) = \frac{1}{20002}
\end{align*}
<Wave hands at the sky, stopped suddenly>
